<body>
    <h1>Root cause analysis (RCA) across industries: Common principles, tra=
ditional methods, commercial offerings landscape and case studies</h1>
    <div class=3D"Section1">
        <p local-id=3D"fe52a95fa255"><strong>Author</strong>: Navita Singh<=
/p>
<p local-id=3D"ac0095d46d26"><strong>Date</strong>: 23 Sep 2026</p>
<p local-id=3D"8d9d5edc34b6"></p>
<p local-id=3D"eaf471b132a5"><strong>RCA is mature in industries with one o=
r more of three characteristics</strong>: <em>high cost of failure, strong =
safety/regulatory requirements, or complex systems where failures can have =
many interacting causes. </em>Example industries include<em> </em>Manufactu=
ring, Automotive, Aerospace &amp; Defense, Healthcare, Pharmaceuticals &amp=
; Life Sciences, Software &amp; IT, Cloud &amp; SaaS, Construction, Energy =
&amp; Utilities, Oil &amp; Gas, Chemical, Mining, Transportation &amp; Rail=
, Logistics &amp; Supply Chain, Telecommunications, Banking &amp; Financial=
 Services, Retail &amp; E-commerce, Food &amp; Beverage, Electronics &amp; =
Semiconductors.</p>
<h2 local-id=3D"2029ed92eca4" id=3D"Rootcauseanalysis(RCA)acrossindustries:=
Commonprinciples,traditionalmethods,commercialofferingslandscapeandcasestud=
ies-Section1.FiveCorePrinciplesofRootcauseanalysis">Section 1. Five Core Pr=
inciples of Root cause analysis</h2>
<p local-id=3D"5689b5bd1021">&nbsp;</p>
<p local-id=3D"dc237c91d1c1">Although RCA methodologies vary by industry, t=
here are <a class=3D"external-link" href=3D"https://instituteprojectmanagem=
ent.com/blog/root-cause-analysis/" rel=3D"nofollow">five foundational princ=
iples</a> that distinguish root cause analysis from solely identifying symp=
toms or assigning responsibility.</p>
<ol start=3D"1" local-id=3D"fe42ad60a9d1">
<li local-id=3D"a9c7795b0554">
<p local-id=3D"b5edd3d076c5"><strong>Causality (Identify the underlying cau=
se, not just the symptom):</strong> RCA seeks to understand the cause and e=
ffect relationships that produced an undesirable outcome. Complex problems =
may have multiple causal and contributing factors rather than a single root=
 cause.</p></li>
</ol>
<ol start=3D"2" local-id=3D"e3941b10f105">
<li local-id=3D"53f45844856d">
<p local-id=3D"bc893aa96aaa"><strong>Specificity (Identify causes that are =
actionable):</strong> A useful root cause must be sufficiently specific to =
support corrective action. Broad explanations such as human error, poor com=
munication, or process failure generally require further investigation. Eff=
ective RCA continues until the analysis identifies underlying causes that c=
an reasonably be addressed through changes to processes, controls, resource=
s, technology, training, or other operating conditions.</p></li>
</ol>
<ol start=3D"3" local-id=3D"2d67a79d3747">
<li local-id=3D"2b14d0ce10df">
<p local-id=3D"6e6a6fb18778"><strong>Evidence (Establish causes through dat=
a):</strong> RCA conclusions should be supported by factual evidence rather=
 than assumptions or intuition. Relevant evidence may include operational d=
ata, project records, timelines, interviews, observations, system logs, pro=
cess measurements, and other documentation. Potential causes should be eval=
uated and, where possible, verified before corrective actions are selected.=
</p></li>
</ol>
<ol start=3D"4" local-id=3D"5d2d3014b4dc">
<li local-id=3D"917b70adc2ae">
<p local-id=3D"4b6ede74b679"><strong>Prevention (Focus on preventing recurr=
ence):</strong> The purpose of RCA is not merely to explain what happened, =
but to use that understanding to prevent the problem from happening again. =
Identified root causes should lead to corrective or preventive actions, fol=
lowed by verification that those actions have reduced or eliminated the und=
erlying source of the problem.</p></li>
</ol>
<ol start=3D"5" local-id=3D"f1f5514dc9a9">
<li local-id=3D"312e703d3a74">
<p local-id=3D"38a4b2d379da"><strong>Systems Thinking (Examine the conditio=
ns that allowed the problem to occur):</strong> RCA looks beyond individual=
 actions to understand the broader system in which an event occurred. Root =
causes may involve processes, procedures, equipment, information flows, org=
anizational structures, controls, resources, or interactions among multiple=
 factors. A systems perspective helps identify vulnerabilities that can be =
addressed through sustainable improvements rather than focusing solely on i=
ndividual error.</p></li>
</ol>
<p local-id=3D"99df7edb1613"></p>
<h2 local-id=3D"de2a55a49214" id=3D"Rootcauseanalysis(RCA)acrossindustries:=
Commonprinciples,traditionalmethods,commercialofferingslandscapeandcasestud=
ies-Section2.EngineeringandconstructionRCAbusinessusecasesbyinsurance-claim=
frequencyandseverity">Section 2. Engineering and construction RCA business =
use cases by insurance-claim frequency and severity</h2>
<p local-id=3D"151d8d028890">The following table compiles share by claim co=
unt and value from <strong>13,599 </strong><a class=3D"external-link" href=
=3D"https://commercial.allianz.com/news-and-insights/reports/engineering-co=
nstruction-claims-insurance-trends.html" rel=3D"nofollow"><strong>engineeri=
ng and construction insurance claims processed by Allianz</strong></a><stro=
ng> from July 2013 through July 2018</strong>, valued at approximately =E2=
=82=AC7.9 billion. It is not a universal construction-industry dataset, how=
ever, can be useful for understanding the types of use cases for RCA encoun=
tered.</p>
<p local-id=3D"b18b60eab8b1">&nbsp;</p>
<p local-id=3D"dca88312368e"><strong>Ranking by insurance-claim frequency a=
nd severity</strong></p>
