<p local-id=3D"a9e2022ccef1">&nbsp;</p>
<h3 local-id=3D"df077acc0eb9" id=3D"Rootcauseanalysis(RCA)acrossindustries:=
Commonprinciples,traditionalmethods,commercialofferingslandscapeandcasestud=
ies-3.1FiveWhys">3.1 Five Whys</h3>
<p local-id=3D"f234d9ad1a6c">The 5 Whys is a structured method for identify=
ing the underlying cause of a problem by repeatedly asking =E2=80=9CWhy?=E2=
=80=9D instead of stopping at the immediate symptom. The process begins by =
assembling people familiar with the issue and clearly defining the problem.=
 The team then asks why the problem occurred and continues questioning each=
 preceding cause, typically five times or until a root cause is identified.=
 Answers should be supported by facts and data rather than assumptions. Onc=
e the root cause is established, the team defines corrective actions, assig=
ns ownership, monitors whether those actions resolve the issue, and documen=
ts the analysis for future learning. <strong>Though the name suggests five =
times, however investigators should continue beyond that till they reach th=
e initial cause in the chain.</strong></p>
<p local-id=3D"095c81772ebd">&nbsp;</p><span class=3D"confluence-embedded-f=
ile-wrapper image-center-wrapper confluence-embedded-manual-size"><img clas=
s=3D"confluence-embedded-image image-center" alt=3D"image-20260923-145256.p=
ng" width=3D"468" loading=3D"lazy" src=3D"f60b924bc69326960f40eeccd28090d30=
8e6cb33ce455a41e39c8eedc793e4bb" data-image-src=3D"https://autodesk.atlassi=
an.net/wiki/download/attachments/1332524964/image-20260923-145256.png?versi=
on=3D1&amp;modificationDate=3D1790175181727&amp;cacheVersion=3D1&amp;api=3D=
v2" data-height=3D"383" data-width=3D"481" data-unresolved-comment-count=3D=
"0" data-linked-resource-id=3D"1332164554" data-linked-resource-version=3D"=
1" data-linked-resource-type=3D"attachment" data-linked-resource-default-al=
ias=3D"image-20260923-145256.png" data-base-url=3D"https://autodesk.atlassi=
an.net/wiki" data-linked-resource-content-type=3D"image/png" data-linked-re=
source-container-id=3D"1332524964" data-linked-resource-container-version=
=3D"22" data-media-id=3D"1fa69956-fe03-4f3f-87ef-7afaa21c2d9c" data-media-t=
ype=3D"file" height=3D"371"></span>
<p local-id=3D"dbc7faebe87c"></p>
<p local-id=3D"a1d06a02b3fe"></p>
<h3 local-id=3D"fb42b5f8df01" id=3D"Rootcauseanalysis(RCA)acrossindustries:=
Commonprinciples,traditionalmethods,commercialofferingslandscapeandcasestud=
ies-3.2Fishbonediagram">&nbsp;3.2 Fishbone diagram</h3>
<p local-id=3D"0c3295740a9b">A fishbone/Ishikawa or cause and effect diagra=
m, helps teams systematically identify and explore the potential causes of =
a problem. The effect is placed at the head of the diagram, while possible =
causes branch out into categories such as people, methods, machines, materi=
als, measurement, and environment. Teams then break these categories into m=
ore specific contributing factors to uncover underlying root causes. By vis=
ually organizing relationships between a problem and its potential causes, =
the fishbone diagram encourages collaborative brainstorming, brings togethe=
r different perspectives, and helps teams move beyond symptoms to identify =
areas where corrective actions can have the greatest impact.</p>
<p local-id=3D"9c814f07068a"></p><span class=3D"confluence-embedded-file-wr=
apper image-center-wrapper confluence-embedded-manual-size"><img class=3D"c=
onfluence-embedded-image image-center" alt=3D"image-20260923-150241.png" wi=
dth=3D"468" loading=3D"lazy" src=3D"734c1afb810fe4a8af201e32cd0bc667cb45a1c=
d221573105804be0cb9cd56a8" data-image-src=3D"https://autodesk.atlassian.net=
/wiki/download/attachments/1332524964/image-20260923-150241.png?version=3D1=
&amp;modificationDate=3D1790175765713&amp;cacheVersion=3D1&amp;api=3Dv2" da=
ta-height=3D"256" data-width=3D"468" data-unresolved-comment-count=3D"0" da=
ta-linked-resource-id=3D"1332558972" data-linked-resource-version=3D"1" dat=
a-linked-resource-type=3D"attachment" data-linked-resource-default-alias=3D=
"image-20260923-150241.png" data-base-url=3D"https://autodesk.atlassian.net=
/wiki" data-linked-resource-content-type=3D"image/png" data-linked-resource=
-container-id=3D"1332524964" data-linked-resource-container-version=3D"22" =
data-media-id=3D"df301462-2724-4d34-ba12-6991060c445f" data-media-type=3D"f=
ile" height=3D"256"></span>
<p local-id=3D"0ba3190455da">&nbsp;</p>
<p local-id=3D"3e7758a7126a"><strong>Industry Examples:</strong></p>
<ul local-id=3D"35a0d74d981e">
<li local-id=3D"5eed15562bac">
<p local-id=3D"2c03dbca4b07">Automotive manufacturing (Ford, identifying tr=
ansmission system inefficiencies; Toyota, identifying root causes of produc=
tion defects)</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"a9fb07f04b0f">
<p local-id=3D"8245f58321ec">Food and beverage (Starbucks, streamlining pro=
duction and identifying supply chain delays) aerospace (NASA, investigating=
 technical anomalies in missions), aviation (Delta Air Lines, proactively p=
reventing flight delays)</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"eea6099981bf">
<p local-id=3D"327aa26dded0">Technology (Microsoft, identifying process ine=
fficiencies; tech startups, improving software debugging and reducing code =
errors) and</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"9992b2cdc569">
<p local-id=3D"93e0255101d4">Electronics manufacturing (Samsung, improving =
product design, innovation, and quality).</p></li>
</ul>
<p local-id=3D"e5b94fcb0d99">&nbsp;</p>
<h3 local-id=3D"5d61ed32f38b" id=3D"Rootcauseanalysis(RCA)acrossindustries:=
Commonprinciples,traditionalmethods,commercialofferingslandscapeandcasestud=
ies-3.3FaultTreeAnalysis(FTA)andFailureModesandEffectsAnalysis(FMEA)">3.3 F=
ault Tree Analysis(FTA) and Failure Modes and Effects Analysis (FMEA)</h3>
<p local-id=3D"bb95031a2fc8">&nbsp;</p>
<p local-id=3D"db7a7f272ae0">Fault Tree Analysis (FTA) is a top down, <stro=
ng>deductive root cause analysis</strong> method used to understand how an =
undesired event or system failure could occur. The analysis begins with a d=
efined top event, such as poor quality concrete pouring, and works downward=
 by identifying the events and conditions that could cause it. These causes=
 are progressively decomposed until the analysis reaches basic or root caus=
es. Logic gates specify how combinations of causes lead to higher level eve=
nts. The resulting tree visually maps potential failure pathways, helping t=
eams identify critical failure points, prioritize corrective actions, and d=
etermine where controls could prevent the failure from occurring.</p>
<p local-id=3D"676b5ae350b5">&nbsp;</p>
<p local-id=3D"7371d3eba1ff">An OR gate means any one of the connected caus=
es is sufficient to produce the event above it. For example, if poor concre=
te quality can result from either an inadequate mix design, improper placem=
ent, or environmental factors independently, these branches would be connec=
ted through an OR gate. An AND gate means all connected causes must occur t=
ogether for the event above them to occur. If a failure only happens when b=
oth inadequate equipment and incorrect operating conditions are present, th=
e two causes would be connected with an AND gate. When exactly one of the c=
onnected events must occur, an exclusive OR or XOR gate is applied.</p>
<p local-id=3D"d084303d82f4">&nbsp;</p>
<p local-id=3D"88ed2de118e9"><strong>Primary causes</strong> are the first =
level causes directly beneath the top event. In the poor quality concrete e=
xample below taken from Mitti, they include insufficient concrete mix desig=
n, improper concrete placement and consolidation, and environmental factors=
. <em>Secondary causes</em> explain why the primary causes occurred and tak=
e the analysis one level deeper. <em>Tertiary causes </em>go another level =
deeper toward fundamental or actionable root causes. In the construction ex=
ample, these include lack of quality assurance processes, poor communicatio=
n between engineers and suppliers, inadequate supervision, failure to follo=
w industry standards, and failure to implement measures for adverse weather=
. The decomposition can continue beyond three levels until meaningful basic=
 or root causes are reached.</p>
<p local-id=3D"95bc8b978825"></p>
<p local-id=3D"b703c6075a95">&nbsp;</p><span class=3D"confluence-embedded-f=
ile-wrapper image-center-wrapper confluence-embedded-manual-size"><img clas=
s=3D"confluence-embedded-image image-center" alt=3D"image-20260923-150732.p=
ng" width=3D"468" loading=3D"lazy" src=3D"4686bce6819ec1d0255a5bd6242c9fe91=
9b1a8df40a8fdf566ba6f6290f90af8" data-image-src=3D"https://autodesk.atlassi=
an.net/wiki/download/attachments/1332524964/image-20260923-150732.png?versi=
on=3D1&amp;modificationDate=3D1790176057753&amp;cacheVersion=3D1&amp;api=3D=
v2" data-height=3D"433" data-width=3D"468" data-unresolved-comment-count=3D=
"0" data-linked-resource-id=3D"1331320153" data-linked-resource-version=3D"=
