ype=3D"file" height=3D"317"></span>
<p local-id=3D"3e344331d1ae">&nbsp;</p>
<h3 local-id=3D"29ab495d3e01" id=3D"Rootcauseanalysis(RCA)acrossindustries:=
Commonprinciples,traditionalmethods,commercialofferingslandscapeandcasestud=
ies-3.4ParetoAnalysis">3.4 Pareto Analysis</h3>
<p local-id=3D"9f8c99bec192">&nbsp;</p>
<p local-id=3D"4c6c5fa2c3aa">Pareto charts help teams identify and prioriti=
ze the <strong>small number of causes responsible for the majority of probl=
ems</strong>, based on the Pareto or 80/20 principle. First, define the pro=
blem and collect data on the frequency of potential causes over a specified=
 period. Next, tally occurrences, sort the causes from most to least freque=
nt, and display them as bars. Add a cumulative percentage line to show whic=
h causes collectively account for at least 80% of the occurrences. Teams ca=
n then focus RCA efforts on the highest contributing categories, investigat=
e those categories in greater detail, identify their underlying causes, and=
 prioritize corrective actions where they can have the greatest impact.</p>
<p local-id=3D"8524c7082cad">&nbsp;</p>
<h2 local-id=3D"dc9d4b21ce7e" id=3D"Rootcauseanalysis(RCA)acrossindustries:=
Commonprinciples,traditionalmethods,commercialofferingslandscapeandcasestud=
ies-Section4.CommercialproductsforRCA">Section 4. Commercial products for R=
CA</h2>
<p local-id=3D"8019d4584155">&nbsp;</p>
<h3 local-id=3D"2b855f26d0f0" id=3D"Rootcauseanalysis(RCA)acrossindustries:=
Commonprinciples,traditionalmethods,commercialofferingslandscapeandcasestud=
ies-4.1TapRooT=C2=AE">4.1 TapRooT=C2=AE</h3>
<ul local-id=3D"061f667d91e5">
<li local-id=3D"0cf78160adbb">
<p local-id=3D"734eee59f3f2"><strong>Root cause analysis problem</strong>: =
TapRooT addresses the challenge of moving beyond the immediate cause of a c=
onstruction or industrial incident to identify the underlying organizationa=
l, human performance, equipment, procedural, and management system factors =
that allowed it to occur. It is primarily used for safety incidents, near m=
isses, equipment failures, quality events, and proactive risk investigation=
s.</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"6b2bfc8a4646">
<p local-id=3D"38efda3fabe8"><strong>Data processed</strong>: TapRooT is pr=
imarily investigator and evidence driven. It processes incident facts, obse=
rvations, interviews, documents, equipment information, timelines, and othe=
r evidence collected during an investigation. Its structured Root Cause Tre=
e=C2=AE and associated tools help investigators classify causal factors, de=
termine root causes, develop corrective actions, and identify recurring pat=
terns across investigations.</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"74808083d692">
<p local-id=3D"2389170fced9"><strong>Client coverage</strong>: TapRooT serv=
es construction, manufacturing, energy, utilities, nuclear, mining, aviatio=
n, and other high-risk industries. Publicly referenced users include Intel,=
 Arkema, Oceaneering, and Air Canada Maintenance, with customers across Nor=
th America, Europe, Asia, and Australia.</p></li>
</ul>
<h3 local-id=3D"6b0aa89f04ac" id=3D"Rootcauseanalysis(RCA)acrossindustries:=
Commonprinciples,traditionalmethods,commercialofferingslandscapeandcasestud=
ies-4.2SmartPM&amp;OracleP6">4.2 SmartPM &amp; Oracle P6</h3>
<p local-id=3D"32d2ecfdf562">&nbsp;</p>
<p local-id=3D"4551cf4bbdf6"><strong>Oracle Primavera P6</strong> is a wide=
ly used construction project scheduling and controls platform that allows t=
eams to create and maintain detailed schedules containing activities, miles=
tones, durations, dependencies, critical paths, resources, and progress. As=
 a project progresses, teams update actual dates, remaining durations, and =
other schedule information. P6 supports baselines and schedule versions tha=
t allow teams to compare current performance with earlier plans and, when a=
uditing is configured, can record detailed changes to schedule fields. This=
 historical schedule data can then be analyzed by tools such as SmartPM to =
identify the underlying drivers of project delays.</p>
<p local-id=3D"1e8c051cd5b9">&nbsp;</p>
<ul local-id=3D"4268f4ee6a35">
<li local-id=3D"01d09f0d6d92">
<p local-id=3D"e4381d57e239"><strong>Root cause analysis problem</strong>: =
SmartPM focuses on determining <em><strong>why construction projects and mi=
lestones are falling behind schedule and identifying the activities</strong=
></em>, logic changes, execution problems, or other events responsible for =
schedule slippage. Its analytics help teams distinguish symptoms from the u=
nderlying causes driving changes in project completion dates, supporting sc=
hedule recovery, cost control, and delay or claims analysis.</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"fb28858c3474">
<p local-id=3D"9804e304d04f"><strong>Data processed</strong>: SmartPM prima=
rily analyzes structured CPM schedule data, including project baselines, su=
ccessive schedule updates, activity dates, durations, dependencies, critica=
l paths, constraints, milestones, float, and schedule changes. It supports =
schedule information from systems such as Primavera P6 and Microsoft Projec=
t and integrates with construction platforms including Procore and Autodesk=
.</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"b39fac95190f">
<p local-id=3D"b18b7d55fde5"><strong>Client coverage</strong>: SmartPM is p=
urpose built for construction and serves general contractors, owners, devel=
opers, and project controls teams. Publicly referenced customers include JE=
 Dunn, Ferrovial, Manhattan Construction, Barton Malow, Suffolk, and AECOM.=
 Its industry analysis has covered more than 120,000 CPM schedules.</p></li=
>
</ul>
<h3 local-id=3D"c1190dadc5f2" id=3D"Rootcauseanalysis(RCA)acrossindustries:=
Commonprinciples,traditionalmethods,commercialofferingslandscapeandcasestud=
ies-4.3SologicCauselink">4.3 Sologic Causelink</h3>
<ul local-id=3D"450f9c2dcd70">
<li local-id=3D"cd74709d27ba">
<p local-id=3D"f398c1c94868"><strong>Root cause analysis problem</strong>: =
Causelink addresses the challenge of conducting complex, evidence-based inv=
estigations consistently across teams, projects, and locations. It enables =
organizations to determine what happened, map causal relationships, validat=
e potential causes against available evidence, define corrective actions, a=
nd preserve investigation knowledge. Relevant use cases include constructio=
n safety incidents, quality failures, equipment problems, operational disru=
ptions, and recurring systemic issues.</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"89ea5f16e431">
<p local-id=3D"c938fb3d1e58"><strong>Data processed</strong>: Causelink pri=
marily processes human curated investigation information, including event t=
imelines, observations, interviews, notes, photographs, videos, documents, =
and other supporting evidence. It organizes this information using cause an=
d effect charts, 5 Whys, Ishikawa diagrams, and FMEA methodologies. Complet=
ed investigations can also be searched and compared to identify recurring c=
auses and broader organizational patterns.</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"68b866a5f4e1">
<p local-id=3D"1fea7b09c28f"><strong>Client coverage</strong>: Sologic serv=
es organizations across construction, engineering, manufacturing, energy, h=
ealthcare, and other industries. A notable construction and engineering cus=
tomer is Quanta Services, which has used Causelink Enterprise across decent=
ralized operating units to standardize RCA practices and identify common ca=
uses across the organization.</p></li>
</ul>
<h3 local-id=3D"dff1db9c6a75" id=3D"Rootcauseanalysis(RCA)acrossindustries:=
Commonprinciples,traditionalmethods,commercialofferingslandscapeandcasestud=
ies-4.4Acerta.ai">4.4 Acerta.ai</h3>
<ul local-id=3D"259ba883e48a">
<li local-id=3D"e7a636c2cd95">
<p local-id=3D"d448d8df0a75"><strong>Root cause analysis problem</strong>: =
Acerta.ai focuses on identifying the manufacturing conditions responsible f=
or product defects, test failures, process anomalies, scrap, rework, and re=
duced first time through yield. Complex manufacturing processes can contain=
 thousands of interacting variables across machines and production stages, =
making manual RCA difficult. Acerta.ai applies machine learning to identify=
 and rank the process signals most strongly associated with abnormal outcom=
es and trace quality problems back to upstream production conditions.</p></=
li>
<li local-id=3D"33e3b0f2459a">
<p local-id=3D"c1744c942632"><strong>Data processed</strong>: Acerta.ai pro=
cesses high volume manufacturing and quality data, including machine and se=
nsor signals, process parameters, tolerances, test results, product attribu=
tes, failure codes, and serialized part genealogy. Data can originate from =
PLCs, MES environments, test systems, and other production sources, allowin=
g relationships to be analyzed across stations, production lines, and facil=
ities.</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"dc78c02e8670">
<p local-id=3D"4a78764a9808"><strong>Client coverage</strong>: Acerta.ai is=
 concentrated in complex discrete manufacturing, particularly automotive OE=
Ms and Tier 1 suppliers. Publicly referenced customers and users include Fo=
rd, BMW, Dana, Linamar, ZF, BorgWarner, and Ballard, with applications cove=
ring components such as engines, axles, powertrain systems, and electric ve=
hicle batteries.</p></li>
</ul>
<h3 local-id=3D"8821255e21a6" id=3D"Rootcauseanalysis(RCA)acrossindustries:=
Commonprinciples,traditionalmethods,commercialofferingslandscapeandcasestud=
ies-4.5Planera">4.5 Planera</h3>
<p local-id=3D"0c0c28b67c5b">&nbsp;</p>
<p local-id=3D"4e65ddde4c9e"><strong>Link</strong>: <a class=3D"external-li=
nk" href=3D"https://www.planera.io/post/delay-analysis-in-construction" rel=
=3D"nofollow">Planera: How to Conduct Delay Analysis for Construction Proje=
cts: Methods, Claims and Best Practices</a></p>
<ul local-id=3D"1df7339c929e">
<li local-id=3D"b7195cab8213">
<p local-id=3D"bc94a0ca81e9"><strong>Schedule risk and root cause analysis =
problem</strong>: Planera focuses on identifying the activities, dependenci=
es, constraints, and resource conditions responsible for construction sched=
ule delays and project risk. Large construction schedules can contain thous=
ands of interconnected activities, making it difficult for project teams to=
 determine which upstream events are driving changes to critical paths, com=
pletion dates, or downstream work. Planera combines Critical Path Method (C=
PM) scheduling with AI-powered analysis to surface schedule risks, identify=
 activities driving delays, trace schedule impacts through predecessor and =
successor relationships, and run =E2=80=9Cwhat-if=E2=80=9D scenarios to ass=
ess potential corrective actions. Its AI scheduling assistant, Manny, allow=
s users to interrogate project schedules in natural language, generate anal=
yses and reports, and identify potential schedule issues.</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"d4a95b008acb">
<p local-id=3D"bae5f015687c"><strong>Data processed</strong>: Planera proce=
sses construction planning and project execution data, including activities=
, durations, milestones, predecessor and successor relationships, calendars=
, constraints, planned and actual dates, remaining durations, critical and =
float paths, labor and equipment resources, baselines, progress updates, va=
riance reason codes, and schedule versions. The platform can analyze this i=
nformation through its CPM engine, schedule quality checks, dashboards, Mon=
te Carlo risk simulation, and AI assistant, allowing project teams to evalu=
ate schedule health, understand changes over time, identify resource or sch=
edule bottlenecks, and assess the potential impact of delays across a proje=
ct.</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"e3699c758888">
<p local-id=3D"df1fc3538068"><strong>Client coverage</strong>: Planera is c=
oncentrated in the construction industry, serving general contractors and p=
roject teams across preconstruction, project execution, pursuits, and enter=
prise portfolio management. The company states that its platform is used by=
 more than 100 builders. Publicly referenced customers and users include Ba=
lfour Beatty, Barton Malow, Granite Construction, Hensel Phelps, Ryan Compa=
nies, Brasfield &amp; Gorrie, Garney Construction, Big-D Construction, Stac=
y Witbeck, Harkins, and Swinerton. Applications span project scheduling, fi=
eld-to-master schedule coordination, schedule quality and compliance, resou=
rce planning, progress tracking, risk analysis, and portfolio-level schedul=
e visibility.</p></li>
</ul>
<p local-id=3D"090b27dc2171"></p>
