ias=3D"image-20260923-172149.png" data-base-url=3D"https://autodesk.atlassi=
an.net/wiki" data-linked-resource-content-type=3D"image/png" data-linked-re=
source-container-id=3D"1332524964" data-linked-resource-container-version=
=3D"22" data-media-id=3D"7c8b7e25-d58b-4e00-9f42-3640284d6c96" data-media-t=
ype=3D"file" height=3D"576"></span>
<p local-id=3D"5bab140dc13f">&nbsp;</p>
<h3 local-id=3D"69c293e3a5db" id=3D"Rootcauseanalysis(RCA)acrossindustries:=
Commonprinciples,traditionalmethods,commercialofferingslandscapeandcasestud=
ies-5.4Scheduledelayanalysismethods">5.4 Schedule delay analysis methods</h=
3>
<p local-id=3D"9e0ee0628cbd">Link: <a class=3D"external-link" href=3D"https=
://www.planera.io/post/delay-analysis-in-construction" rel=3D"nofollow">Pla=
nera: How to Conduct Delay Analysis for Construction Projects: Methods, Cla=
ims and Best Practices</a></p>
<ul local-id=3D"ae64adeb4390">
<li local-id=3D"d70c3af9c5cd">
<p local-id=3D"ece7060c6a38"><strong>As-Planned vs. As-Built:</strong> Comp=
ares the <strong>original baseline schedule with actual project performance=
</strong> to identify overall delay. Simple and cost-effective, but limited=
 in determining the specific causes of delay.</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"a6a86ab41bf5">
<p local-id=3D"e9f09c776dc4"><strong>Impacted As-Planned:</strong> Adds <st=
rong>delay events to the original baseline</strong> to estimate their poten=
tial impact on project completion. Easy to model, but does not reflect actu=
al progress or subsequent schedule changes.</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"669ad7a107d6">
<p local-id=3D"57c3497e9a1b"><strong>Time Impact Analysis (TIA):</strong> I=
nserts specific <strong>delay events (fragnets) into the contemporaneous CP=
M schedule</strong> to measure their impact on completion. Strong for event=
-specific analysis but requires reliable schedule updates and detailed reco=
rds.</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"733f02998418">
<p local-id=3D"e1c50a7fce76"><strong>Windows Analysis:</strong> Breaks the =
project into <strong>defined time periods</strong> to evaluate how delays a=
nd the critical path changed over time. Well suited to complex projects but=
 requires significant data and analysis.</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"ab705d6a28a2">
<p local-id=3D"0b8252febb14"><strong>Collapsed As-Built (But-For):</strong>=
 Starts with the <strong>completed as-built schedule and removes selected d=
elay events</strong> to estimate when the project would have finished witho=
ut them. Useful for post-completion analysis but highly dependent on reliab=
le as-built records.</p></li>
</ul>
<p local-id=3D"402c89285057"></p>
<h2 local-id=3D"22bbcd440c30" id=3D"Rootcauseanalysis(RCA)acrossindustries:=
Commonprinciples,traditionalmethods,commercialofferingslandscapeandcasestud=
ies-Section6.CausalinferenceandRCA">Section 6. Causal inference and RCA</h2=
>
<p local-id=3D"bf129b8080fa">Link: <a class=3D"external-link" data-card-app=
earance=3D"inline" href=3D"https://www.glyphmath.com/articles/causal-infere=
nce-root-cause/" local-id=3D"e4b54a07875b" rel=3D"nofollow">https://www.gly=
phmath.com/articles/causal-inference-root-cause/</a></p>
<p local-id=3D"40305576dbfe">&nbsp;</p>
<ul local-id=3D"50de47395e88">
<li local-id=3D"088d36914c9b">
<p local-id=3D"198788e56c20"><strong>RCA vs. causal inference</strong>: <st=
rong>Causal inference</strong> is the process of determining the independen=
t effect of a cause on an outcome by asking how the outcome would change if=
 the cause were changed, rather than just observing their correlation<stron=
g>. Root Cause Analysis (RCA) is broader and more action oriented: it asks =
why a specific failure occurred and what intervention can prevent it from r=
ecurring</strong>. In complex systems, there may be multiple interacting ca=
uses rather than a single root cause, so identifying a causal relationship =
alone may not identify the best corrective action.</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"b04ed5718e19">
<p local-id=3D"c0233fbc4dc4"><strong>Using causal inference for RCA</strong=
>: Causal inference can strengthen RCA by testing which suspected causes ac=
tually influence the failure outcome. Teams can first develop hypotheses or=
 a causal graph linking potential causes to the failure, then use statistic=
al methods, controlled experiments, natural experiments, or counterfactual =
analysis to estimate what would happen if a suspected cause were changed. F=
or example, rather than concluding that high temperature causes manufacturi=
ng defects because the two are correlated, causal analysis can test whether=
 changing temperature would actually reduce defects while controlling for o=
ther process variables. Causal inference therefore provides evidence for ca=
usal links, while RCA determines which interventions should be implemented.=
</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"6b1c58a97800">
<p local-id=3D"a2175f35d896"><strong>Data needed for causal inference</stro=
ng>: Causal inference generally requires historical observational or experi=
mental data containing the failure outcome, suspected causal variables, and=
 relevant confounding variables. For manufacturing RCA, this could include =
machine and sensor readings, process parameters, material characteristics, =
timestamps, product genealogy, operator or shift information, environmental=
 conditions, maintenance events, and quality or defect outcomes. Ideally, t=
he dataset contains sufficient variation in operating conditions and exampl=
es of both successful and failed outcomes. Domain knowledge is also importa=
nt for constructing the causal graph and deciding which variables may be ca=
uses, confounders, mediators, or consequences.</p></li>
</ul>
